This is describe the composite characters of the Extended TRON Code. (This
is currently a draft and may be changed in future; others may write
comments about it if you have something to write, please.)

Composite character groups may be defined; each group is limited to
one plane, and the character codes within a group are designated as
Beginning Codes, Middle Codes, and Ending Codes (only one each; a
single code cannot be more than one of these). Also, each character
code can only belong to up to one group (although a character code
might not belong to any group, in which case it is a Isolated Code).

A composite character sequence of two or more character codes that all
belong to the same group, of which the first is a Beginning Code, the last
is a Ending Code, and the rest are Middle Codes. Nothing else is allowed
between these codes, including control characters and plane shifts.

Composite character rendering can be enabled or disabled (usually it will
be disabled if Reveal Codes is active or enabled otherwise), and can be
done independently from kerns and ligatures (although, probably you could
not control kerns independently from ligatures; either both are enabled or
both are disabled). When the composite character rendering is enabled, then
a composite character as defined above is considered as a single character,
for moving cursor, deleting, etc; otherwise, they are separate characters.

A font will contains additional information based on this:

* The font will contain the definition of composite character groups which
are included in that font.

* Each character will also have a "composite width" flag; if the flag is
set, then the width is considered to be zero when measuring the width of a
string if composite character rendering is enabled.

* Each kern will have a "composite kern" flag; if the flag is set, then the
kern is ignored except for measuring the width of a string when composite
character rendering is enabled.

* For each composite character group in the font, also a program (using
some simple and very limited VM code) for converting the sequence of
character codes into a set of glyphs to draw (which may include private
glyphs) and the relative positions of the glyphs, using the measurements
of other characters in the font as the units of measurement.

Note that the font will also have to specify text direction, which can be
any direction, and may have different glyphs for different directions. The
text direction is set by separate control characters and/or formatting
codes which are not a part of the character set and not set by the font.
So, the advancements in the font are "forward" in whatever text direction
is the current text direction, which is not necessarily to the right.

(Combining characters and stuff like that are not used; European alphabets
will have separate character codes for accented vs non-accented alphabets
andare not considered to be composite characters. However, composite
characters might possibly be useful for Cangjie, Blissymbols, etc.)
